---

Every year, thousands of Nairobi County graduates enter a job market that has no room for them — not because they lack talent, but because they lack market-driven skills and the connections to put them to work. Bill Llia's **Nairobi County Youth Skills and Enterprise Program** aims to change that for 500 young people in the first year alone.

## What each participant gets

- **Three months** of hands-on vocational training at a certified institution in high-demand fields — digital skills, agribusiness, renewable energy installation, and professional services
- **A paid apprenticeship** placement with a local business or government department
- **A mentorship pairing** with an established professional who provides guidance, networking, and accountability
- **A startup grant of up to KES 50,000** for graduates who launch their own businesses

"We have thousands of talented young people in Nairobi County who are ready to work," Bill said at the launch. "What they lack is not ability — it is opportunity. This program will close that gap."

## Built for the real market

The program runs through partnerships with TVET institutions, local businesses, and community organizations. An advisory board of youth representatives, business leaders, and education experts oversees the curriculum and job placement outcomes.

"The worst thing we can do is train young people for jobs that do not exist," Bill added. "Every course in this program is designed based on real market demand. We are not teaching skills for the sake of it — we are building a pipeline to employment and entrepreneurship."

## What this means for Nairobi's youth

If you are a young person in Nairobi County with skills to learn and a future to build, this program determines whether your next step is another year of frustration — or a real shot at a career. The next Governor decides whether programs like this get funded, staffed, and scaled. Do not sit this election out.
